\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A system of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 QCA molecules acts as a binary wire.
 A driver cell is labeled as cell 0,
 and the 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 circuit cells are labeled 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in\left\{ 1,2,\ldots,N\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Each cell is a distance 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 from its nearest neighbors.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:DrivenBinaryWire"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
To simplify the problem,
 we will assume that only nearest neighbors interact
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Thus,
 in this limit,
 only cell 0 is directly influenced by the driver,
 drives a bias given in Equations (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Delta_Pdrv"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:KinkGeometricConstant"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) as the following function of driver polarization,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{0}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta\left(P_{0}\right)=\left(1-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)\frac{q_{e}^{2}}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}a}P_{0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
If cell 0 has tunneling energy 
\begin_inset Formula $-\gamma$
\end_inset

,
 then we write its Hamiltonian as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{H}_{0}=-\gamma\hat{\sigma}_{x}^{\left(0\right)}+\frac{\Delta}{2}\hat{\sigma}_{z}^{\left(0\right)},\label{eq:HamiltonianDrivenCell}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we have added the superscript 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(0\right)$
\end_inset

 to indicate that these Pauli operators are for the Hilbert space of cell 0,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 is the term that describes the effect of the driver on cell 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The bold notation and hat notation both signify matrices.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
For cells 
\begin_inset Formula $1,2,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

,
 there is no driver bias,
 so we write the Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{k}$
\end_inset

 for the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-th cell in a way that describes charge tunneling in the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-th cell,
 but no bias:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{H}_{k}=-\gamma\hat{\sigma}_{x}^{\left(k\right)}\label{eq:HamiltonianRespondentCell}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
By definition,
 this operator acts only on the Hilbert space of the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-th molecule,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{k}$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Before we can sum the various 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{k}$
\end_inset

,
 we need to expand each one into the Hilbert space of the full system
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The composite Hilbert space is a tensor product of individual Hilbert spaces :
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{H}=\mathcal{H}_{N-1}\otimes\mathcal{H}_{N-2}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathcal{H}_{1}\otimes\mathcal{H}_{0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Hamiltonian for cell 0 is expanded to the full Hilbert space as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{1}_{N-1}\otimes\hat{1}_{N-2}\otimes\cdots\otimes\hat{1}_{2}\otimes\hat{1}_{1}\otimes\hat{H}_{0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The identity operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{1}_{k}$
\end_inset

 from Hilbert spaces 
\begin_inset Formula $k\neq0$
\end_inset

 indicate that no description of other QCA molecules is included here
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The Hamiltonian for cell 1 is expanded to the full Hilbert space as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{1}_{N-1}\otimes\hat{1}_{N-2}\otimes\cdots\otimes\hat{1}_{2}\otimes\hat{H}_{1}\otimes\hat{1}_{0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Hamiltonian for cell 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is expanded to the full Hilbert space 
\begin_inset Formula $as$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{1}_{N}\otimes\hat{1}_{N-1}\otimes\cdots\otimes\hat{1}_{k+1}\otimes\hat{H}_{k}\otimes\hat{1}_{k-1}\otimes\cdots\otimes\hat{1}_{2}\otimes\hat{1}_{1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Formally,
 we would combine the expanded Hamiltonians into 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

,
 the non-interacting or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

free
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Hamiltonian:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\hat{H}_{0} & =\hat{1}_{N-1}\otimes\hat{1}_{N-2}\otimes\cdots\otimes\hat{1}_{2}\otimes\hat{1}_{1}\otimes\hat{H}_{0}\\
 & \quad+\hat{1}_{N}\otimes\hat{1}_{N-1}\otimes\cdots\otimes\hat{1}_{3}\otimes\hat{H}_{2}\otimes\hat{1}_{1}\\
 & \quad+\cdots\\
 & \quad+\hat{H}_{N}\otimes\hat{1}_{N-1}\otimes\cdots\otimes\hat{1}_{2}\otimes\hat{1}_{1}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Since each operator is from a two-dimensional Hilbert space,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{free}}$
\end_inset

 is described by a 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{N}\times2^{N}$
\end_inset

 matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It is common practice to simplify our notation and ignore the identity operators,
 leaving them implicit rather than explicit,
 so that we write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{\text{free}}=\hat{H}_{0}+\hat{H}_{1}+\cdots+\hat{H}_{N-1}=\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\hat{H}_{N-1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{free}}$
\end_inset

 is said to be non-interacting because no description is included here of interactions between molecules
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{free}}$
\end_inset

 describes each molecule as if it were in isolation
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
The Interaction Hamiltonian,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{int}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
To include interactions in the complete Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 for the full circuit,
 we add an interaction term,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{int}}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}=\hat{H}_{\text{free}}+\hat{H}_{\text{int }}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The interaction term 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{int}}$
\end_inset

 is a diagonal operator in the computational basis:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\hat{H}_{\text{int}} & =\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
U_{0}\\
 & U_{1}\\
 &  & \ddots\\
 &  &  & U_{2^{N}-1}
\end{array}\right]\\
 & =\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\Braket{0|\hat{H}_{\text{int}}|0}\\
 & \Braket{1|\hat{H}_{\text{int}}|1}\\
 &  & \ddots\\
 &  &  & \Braket{2^{N}-1|\hat{H}_{\text{int}}|2^{N}-1}
\end{array}\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{x}$
\end_inset

 is an integer encoding a product of individual bit states:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{x}=\ket{x_{N}}\otimes\ket{x_{N-1}}\otimes\cdots\otimes\ket{x_{2}}\otimes\ket{x_{1}}=\ket{x_{N}x_{N-1}\cdots x_{2}x_{1}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Explicitly,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\ket{0} & =\ket{00\cdots000}\\
\ket{1} & =\ket{00\cdots001}\\
\ket{2} & =\ket{00\cdots010}\\
\ket{3} & =\ket{00\cdots011}\\
 & \vdots\\
\ket{2^{N}-1} & =\ket{11\cdots111}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{int}}$
\end_inset

 is diagonal,
 we interpret its elements as contributions to the system potential energy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Thus:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{0|\hat{H}_{\text{int}}|0}$
\end_inset

 is the energy of interaction between the cells when all cells are in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 state
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{1|\hat{H}_{\text{int}}|1}$
\end_inset

 is the energy of interaction between the cells when all cells are in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 state,
 except for cell 0,
 which is in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 state
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{2|\hat{H}_{\text{int}}|2}$
\end_inset

 is the energy of interaction between the cells when all cells are in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 state,
 except for cell 1,
 which is in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 state,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
and so on,
 until we reach 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{2^{N}-1|\hat{H}_{\text{int}}|2^{N}-1}$
\end_inset

,
 which is the energy of interaction between the cells when all cells are in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 state
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The energies 
\begin_inset Formula $\braket{k|\hat{H}_{\text{int}}|k}$
\end_inset

 can be calculated from electrostat
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
ics by summing the energy of assembly of the charges given a particular circuit configuration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Another way to determine the energies is through symmetry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We will now illustrate this with an example
\end_layout

