\begin_layout Enumerate
In the limit of 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

,
 find the ground state of the system.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{k}>0$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>Here,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}$
\end_inset

 becomes diagonal:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}=\hat{H}_{\text{free}}+\hat{H}_{\text{int}}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\frac{\Delta}{2}+\frac{E_{k}}{2} & 0 & 0 & 0\\
0 & -\frac{\Delta}{2}-\frac{E_{k}}{2} & 0 & 0\\
0 & 0 & \frac{\Delta}{2}-\frac{E_{k}}{2} & 0\\
0 & 0 & 0 & -\frac{\Delta}{2}+\frac{E_{k}}{2}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Thus,
 the eigenenergies are the diagonal elements.
 The lowest-energy eigenvalue is easy to spot:
 it's the second element,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Braket{01|\hat{H}|01}=-\frac{\Delta}{2}-\frac{E_{k}}{2},
\]

\end_inset

corresponding to the state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{10}$
\end_inset

,
 wherein cell 0 is in state 1 due to the driving bias 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta>0$
\end_inset

,
 and the two cells are antialigned.
 If the driving bias is small,
 i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ll E_{k}$
\end_inset

,
 the states 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{01}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{10}$
\end_inset

 are very close in energy,
 but state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{01}$
\end_inset

 is lower,
 nonetheless.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

#+html:
 </details>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the ground state of the system in the limit of 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 and with no external bias,
 i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>When the driving bias is removed (
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

),
 the Hamiltonian reduces to
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}=\hat{H}_{\text{free}}+\hat{H}_{\text{int}}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\frac{E_{k}}{2} & 0 & 0 & 0\\
0 & -\frac{E_{k}}{2} & 0 & 0\\
0 & 0 & -\frac{E_{k}}{2} & 0\\
0 & 0 & 0 & \frac{E_{k}}{2}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

the states 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{10}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{01}$
\end_inset

 have the same energy,
 
\begin_inset Formula $-E_{k}/2$
\end_inset

.
 The system will have two degenerate ground state energies (lowest eigenenergies),
 so that any normalized quantum superposition of these two states has the lowest energy.
 Two straight-forward examples are
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\ket{01}\pm\ket{10}\right).
\]

\end_inset

