\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
Exercise 5.7
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Find the Hamiltonian for the driven pair of QCA cells in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:DrivenBinaryWireN2"

\end_inset

.
 For simplicity,
 we consider only nearest-neighbor interactions.
 That is,
 the driver only affects cell 0,
 so that the Hamiltonian for cell 0 is as before:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{0}=-\gamma\hat{\sigma}_{x}+\frac{\Delta}{2}\hat{\sigma}_{z}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
-\frac{\Delta}{2} & -\gamma\\
-\gamma & \frac{\Delta}{2}
\end{array}\right].
\]

\end_inset

The Hamiltonian for cell 1 will not include the driver term proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{1}=-\gamma\hat{\sigma}_{x}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & -\gamma\\
-\gamma & 0
\end{array}\right].
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Before we can combine these into the free Hamiltonian,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{free}}$
\end_inset

,
 we must expand each into the full Hilbert space,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}=\mathcal{H}_{0}\otimes\mathcal{H}_{1}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{\text{free}}=\hat{H}_{0}\otimes\hat{1}_{1}+\hat{1}_{0}\otimes\hat{H}_{1}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
-\frac{\Delta}{2} & -\gamma & -\gamma & 0\\
-\gamma & -\frac{\Delta}{2} & 0 & -\gamma\\
-\gamma & 0 & \frac{\Delta}{2} & -\gamma\\
0 & -\gamma & -\gamma & \frac{\Delta}{2}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

The interaction term may be written in matrix form as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\hat{H}_{\text{int}} & =\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\Braket{00|\hat{H}_{\text{int}}|00}\\
 & \Braket{01|\hat{H}_{\text{int}}|01}\\
 &  & \Braket{10|\hat{H}_{\text{int}}|10}\\
 &  &  & \Braket{11|\hat{H}_{\text{int}}|11}
\end{array}\right]\\
 & =\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
U_{00}\\
 & U_{01}\\
 &  & U_{10}\\
 &  &  & U_{10}
\end{array}\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{\beta\alpha}=\Braket{\beta\alpha|\hat{H}|\beta\alpha}$
\end_inset

 is the potential energy of interaction between cell 0 in state 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and cell 1 in state 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{00}$
\end_inset

 is the energy of interaction between both cells in state 0,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{01}$
\end_inset

 has cell 0 in state 1 but cell 1 in state 0.
 Additionally,
 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{11}$
\end_inset

 has both cells in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 state,
 but 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{10}$
\end_inset

 has only cell 1 in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 state.
 Thus,
 we can recognize that the difference between 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{00}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{U}_{10}$
\end_inset

 is nothing more than the energy of a kink:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{00}-U_{01}=U_{11}-U_{10}=E_{k}
\]

\end_inset

Furthermore,
 we should expect that 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{01}=U_{10}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{00}=U_{11}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{\text{int}}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
U_{00}\\
 & U_{10}\\
 &  & U_{10}\\
 &  &  & U_{00}
\end{array}\right].
\]

\end_inset

Furthermore,
 we can define the zero of potential energy to be halfway between 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{00}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{10}$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{01}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{10}$
\end_inset

 so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{\text{int}}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\frac{E_{k}}{2}\\
 & -\frac{E_{k}}{2}\\
 &  & -\frac{E_{k}}{2}\\
 &  &  & \frac{E_{k}}{2}
\end{array}\right].
\]

\end_inset

We can see here that the states 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{00}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{11}$
\end_inset

 are a full kink energy above the states 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{01}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{10}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{int}}$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{\text{int}}=-\frac{E_{k}}{2}\hat{\sigma}_{z}\otimes\hat{\sigma}_{z}.
\]

\end_inset

Now,
 we can find the total Hamiltonian,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\hat{H} & =\hat{H}_{\text{free}}+\hat{H}_{\text{int}}\\
 & =\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
-\frac{\Delta}{2}+\frac{E_{k}}{2} & -\gamma & -\gamma & 0\\
-\gamma & -\frac{\Delta}{2}-\frac{E_{k}}{2} & 0 & -\gamma\\
-\gamma & 0 & \frac{\Delta}{2}-\frac{E_{k}}{2} & -\gamma\\
0 & -\gamma & -\gamma & \frac{\Delta}{2}+\frac{E_{k}}{2}
\end{array}\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset

#+html:
 </details>
\end_layout

