\begin_layout Plain Layout

\series bold
Exercise 5.9
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Find the ground state of the system from Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:DrivenBinaryWireN2"

\end_inset

 under the simplifications 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

,
 but now with each cell experiencing internal bias 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{0}$
\end_inset

 and external bias,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{e}$
\end_inset

.
 For now,
 let us combine the internal and external bias as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta_{t}=\Delta_{o}+\Delta_{e}.
\]

\end_inset

This will make our calculations simpler.
 When we want to explore the competition between 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{o}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{e}$
\end_inset

,
 we can add that level of granularity and complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Now,
 both cells have the same Hamiltonian form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{0}=\hat{H}_{1}=-\gamma\hat{\sigma}_{x}+\frac{\Delta_{t}}{2}\hat{\sigma}_{z}
\]

\end_inset

The free Hamiltonian is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{\text{free}}=\hat{H}_{1}\otimes\hat{1}_{0}+\hat{1}_{1}\otimes\hat{H}_{0}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
-\Delta_{t} & -\gamma & -\gamma & 0\\
-\gamma & 0 & 0 & -\gamma\\
-\gamma & 0 & 0 & -\gamma\\
0 & -\gamma & -\gamma & \Delta_{t}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Here,
 the bias 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{t}$
\end_inset

 cancels in the states 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{01}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{10}$
\end_inset

.
 We use the same interaction term 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{int}}$
\end_inset

 as before.
 Thus,
 the total Hamiltonian is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}=\hat{H}_{\text{free}}+\hat{H}_{\text{int}}=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\frac{E_{k}}{2}-\Delta_{t} & -\gamma & -\gamma & 0\\
-\gamma & -\frac{E_{k}}{2} & 0 & -\gamma\\
-\gamma & 0 & -\frac{E_{k}}{2} & -\gamma\\
0 & -\gamma & -\gamma & \frac{E_{k}}{2}+\Delta_{t}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

If we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{k}>\Delta_{t}$
\end_inset

 (the kink energy is greater than the bias 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{t}$
\end_inset

),
 then the ground state energy is 
\begin_inset Formula $-E_{k}/2$
\end_inset

 with eigenstates 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{01}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{10}$
\end_inset

.
 Under these circumstances,
 the ground state will be depolarized:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\psi_{0}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\ket{01}+\ket{10}\right).
\]

\end_inset

If we increase the bias,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{t}$
\end_inset

,
 we make 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{00}$
\end_inset

 become the ground state when 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{k}/2-\Delta_{t}<E_{k}/2$
\end_inset

,
 which means that we need 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{t}>E_{k}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

