\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A neighboring driver molecule provides the classical bias,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

,
 for a row of three QCA cells.
 Fixed neutralizing charge of 
\begin_inset Formula $+q_{e}/2$
\end_inset

 resides at each dot so that with one mobile electron,
 each molecular cell is charge neutral.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:DrivenDQDrowN3"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Consider a three-cell circuit (i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $N=3$
\end_inset

) as in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:DrivenDQDrowN3"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the free Hamiltonian,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{free}}$
\end_inset

,
 for the circuit.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>As before,
 only cell 0 is biased by the driver,
 so we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{0}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
-\frac{\Delta}{2} & -\gamma\\
-\gamma & \frac{\Delta}{2}
\end{array}\right].
\]

\end_inset

The Hamiltonians for cell 1 and cell 2 are of the same form,
 given by Equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:HamiltonianRespondentCell"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{1},\hat{H}_{2}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & -\gamma\\
-\gamma & 0
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

We may explicitly expand the individual Hamiltonian operators into the full Hilbert space and sum them:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{\text{free}}=\mathbf{1}\otimes\mathbf{1}\otimes\hat{H}_{0}+\mathbf{1}\otimes\hat{H}_{1}\otimes\mathbf{1}+\hat{H}_{2}\otimes\mathbf{1}\otimes\mathbf{1}.
\]

\end_inset

More concisely,
 we will write this as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{\text{int}}=\hat{H}_{0}+\hat{H}_{1}+\hat{H}_{2},
\]

\end_inset

with the expansion into the full three-cell Hilbert space left implicit.
 We will not bother writing the matrix representation for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{free}}$
\end_inset

,
 since this is a 
\begin_inset Formula $8\times8$
\end_inset

 matrix (tedious).
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

#+html:
 </details>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the the interaction term,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{H}_{\text{int}}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>To find the interaction Hamiltonian,
 we can include only nearest-neighbor interactions,
 i.e.,
 those of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $-\left(E_{k}/2\right)\hat{\sigma}_{z}^{\left(k+1\right)}\otimes\hat{\sigma}_{z}^{\left(k\right)}$
\end_inset

.
 We could write this as a sum:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{\text{int}}=-\frac{E_{k}}{2}\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\hat{\sigma}_{z}^{\left(k+1\right)}\otimes\hat{\sigma}_{z}^{\left(k\right)}=-\frac{E_{k}}{2}\left(\hat{\sigma}_{z}^{\left(1\right)}\otimes\hat{\sigma}_{z}^{\left(0\right)}+\hat{\sigma}_{z}^{\left(2\right)}\otimes\hat{\sigma}_{z}^{\left(1\right)}\right),
\]

\end_inset

where,
 if we wish to explicitly show the expansion into the full Hilbert space,
 we would write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{\text{int}}=-\frac{E_{k}}{2}\left(\mathbf{1}\otimes\hat{\sigma}_{z}\otimes\hat{\sigma}_{z}+\hat{\sigma}_{z}\otimes\hat{\sigma}_{z}\otimes\mathbf{1}\right).
\]

\end_inset

Here,
 I have neglected to write the superscript Hilbert space labels,
 since the order of the tensor product makes it clear which cells are being treated in each interaction term.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

#+html:
 </details>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+end_note
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

