\begin_layout Plain Layout
Find the ground state of the three-cell system from Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:DrivenDQDrowN3"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 under the simplifications 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

,
 but now with each cell experiencing internal bias 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{0}$
\end_inset

 and external bias,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{e}$
\end_inset

.
 For now,
 let us combine the internal and external bias as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta=\Delta_{o}+\Delta_{e}.
\]

\end_inset

This will make our calculations simpler.
 When we want to explore the competition between 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{o}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{e}$
\end_inset

,
 we can add that level of granularity and complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Now,
 both cells have the same Hamiltonian form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}_{0}=\hat{H}_{1}=\hat{H}_{2}=-\gamma\hat{\sigma}_{x}+\frac{\Delta}{2}\hat{\sigma}_{z}
\]

\end_inset

The free Hamiltonian in the limit of 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=0$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\hat{H}_{\text{free}} & =\hat{H}_{2}\otimes\hat{1}_{1}\otimes\hat{1}_{0}+\hat{1}_{2}\otimes\hat{H}_{1}\otimes\hat{1}_{0}+\hat{1}_{2}\otimes\hat{1}_{1}\otimes\hat{H}_{0}\\
 & =\left[\begin{array}{cccccccc}
-\frac{3\Delta}{2}\\
 & -\frac{\Delta}{2}\\
 &  & -\frac{\Delta}{2}\\
 &  &  & \frac{\Delta}{2}\\
 &  &  &  & -\frac{\Delta}{2}\\
 &  &  &  &  & \frac{\Delta}{2}\\
 &  &  &  &  &  & \frac{\Delta}{2}\\
 &  &  &  &  &  &  & \frac{3\Delta}{2}
\end{array}\right]
\end{align*}

\end_inset

The energy of the isolated cells simply decreases by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta/2$
\end_inset

 for each cell in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 state and increase by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta/2$
\end_inset

 for each cell in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 state.
